speechtext:
1. As the President of the Treasury Board stated in the House on October 24,1989, the present practice of reporting Ministerial travel expenses was established by a Cabinet decision in 1963 and has been followed by every successive government.
2.	The travel expenditures for each Minister is reported in Public Accounts for each fiscal year.
3.	Under new guidelines, announced by the President of the Treasury Board in the House of Commons on October 24, the Comptroller General of Canada will make available on a regular basis:
a)	Travel expenses of each Minister;
b)	The number of times that each Minister used the government aircraft;
c)	The date, the itinerary, and the names of all passengers on each trip in a government aircraft;
d)	The reason for the use of the government aircraft;
e)	The cost of the use of the government aircraft.
The Comptroller General has been directed by the President of the Treasury Board to establish this new reporting system as soon as possible.
4.	Regarding the use of government aircraft during the period from November 1988 to August 1989, 220 trips* were made totalling 1,413.2 hours of flight.
'Figures include Prime Minister, all Ministers, Governor General, and visiting Foreign Heads of State.
